                    round_name_r1:          `Find the Goal`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Design & Modalities`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Measurement Case`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Defend the Budget`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Find the business goal`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You have the strategy deck, the stalled-services data and the current L&D catalogue. The temptation is to promise to “improve training in general.” Resist it. Strategic L&D earns its seat only when it is tied to <strong>a single measurable business goal</strong> — and the board’s #1 priority is the services transition: the win-rate is stuck at <strong>18% vs a 35% target</strong>, and outcome contracts carry <em>22% margin against 14%</em>. Pin the program to a business number, map the capabilities that actually move it (consultative selling, solution scoping, service economics), and prune the catalogue courses that don’t. Then commit to a win-rate lift you can defend.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the program & choose modalities`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the solution against the goal. The expensive default is <strong>three days of classroom for all 1,400</strong> — most of your budget, three lost selling days each, and the least behavior change per euro. Research is blunt: capability builds roughly <em>70% on the job, 20% through coaching, 10% formal</em>. Decide where high-cost coaching earns its keep (the front-line deal teams who move the win-rate), where cheaper digital scales, and what the manager’s job is in making it stick — then size the spend against the <strong>€4.2M budget</strong> without blowing it.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Build the measurement case`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The board does not accept “happy sheets” as evidence. Climb the <strong>Kirkpatrick ladder</strong> from reaction → learning → behavior → <em>business results</em>. Define leading indicators that move first (skill assessments, manager-observed deal behaviors), lagging indicators (win-rate, service margin), and — the question the CFO will ask — a credible <strong>attribution</strong> approach. A trained-vs-untrained cohort comparison isolates the program’s effect; “win-rate rose after we trained” is a story, not evidence. Then ground an ROI in the share of the €11M your evidence can actually claim.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the budget to the board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Three minutes in front of the board and the CFO. Lead with the business case, not the activity. The CFO presses the <strong>€1.4M cut</strong>. Conceding it to look responsible defunds the capability behind an <em>€11M opportunity</em> — being agreeable isn’t strategic. Connect the spend to the margin, show the measurement plan, answer the cut, and commit to the <strong>quarterly business KPIs</strong> and the executive sponsorship and manager release-time the program needs. Then state how firmly you hold that L&D is an investment, not a cost.`,

                    r1_goal_label:          `Anchor the program to a single goal. Which goal do you commit to the board?`,
                    r1_capability_label:    `Which capabilities actually drive that goal? (where you will concentrate the program)`,
                    r1_catalogue_label:     `The legacy catalogue is full of courses irrelevant to the goal. What do you do?`,
                    r1_target_label:        `How much of the 18→35% win-rate gap do you commit to closing this cycle? (percentage points)`,
                    r1_target_readout:      `Win-rate lift you put your name to:`,
                    r1_pts:                 ` pts (18% → `,
                    r1_pct_close:           `%)`,
                    r1_target_hint:         `A credible, evidenced target builds CFO confidence; a vague or over-promised number invites the cut. The board target is 35% (a 17-point lift).`,
