                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_winrate:       `<strong>That’s the anchor a board funds.</strong> The services win-rate is our #1 priority, it’s measurable, and it sits directly upstream of the €11M margin opportunity. Now the conversation is about a business number — not whether people enjoyed a course. Tie the whole program to it.`,
                    coach_r1_completion:    `<strong>You’ve committed to lifting completion from 71% to 90%.</strong> I listen, then I ask: and what business number moves when completion goes up? You don’t have one — which is <em>exactly</em> why the budget is being cut. Completion is an activity metric, not value. What single business metric will this program move, and by how much?`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>Higher satisfaction than 4.6/5 is a happy sheet, not a strategy.</strong> A board doesn’t fund a more enjoyable course catalogue out of an €11M margin opportunity. Which business metric does a more satisfied learner move — and how would I see it in the numbers?`,
                    coach_r1_broad:         `<strong>“Upskill the whole organization” is the activity trap dressed up as ambition.</strong> Spread across 5,200 people with no single target, it moves no metric I can point to. Strategic L&D starts from one business goal and works backward. Pick the number.`,
                    coach_r1_compliance:    `<strong>Keeping the catalogue current isn’t the goal.</strong> Compliance and feature training don’t build consultative selling — the capability the transition actually needs. You’ve anchored to a business number; now point the capability map at it.`,
                    coach_r1_generic:       `<strong>Generic skills for everyone won’t move the win-rate.</strong> The transition needs consultative selling, solution scoping and service economics in the 1,400-person commercial and field force — not leadership courses for all 5,200. Concentrate the capability where the goal lives.`,
                    coach_r1_overpromise:   `<strong>Committing to nearly the full 35% in one cycle is fantasy, and a board knows it.</strong> Over-promise and miss, and you hand the CFO the cut next year with interest. Put your name to a credible, evidenced stretch — not the moon.`,
                    coach_r1_lowball:       `<strong>A 1–4 point lift reads as no ambition.</strong> If that’s all the program will move, the CFO will reasonably ask why it costs €4.2M. Commit to a credible stretch you can evidence — enough to matter against the €11M.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That’s a 70-20-10 design, not a classroom reflex.</strong> The bulk of capability builds on the job and through coaching and manager reinforcement, with formal instruction as the smaller, targeted slice. More behavior change per euro, and the field force keeps selling. This is what moves the win-rate.`,
                    coach_r2_classroom:     `<strong>Three days of classroom for 1,400 people — that’s most of your budget and three lost selling days each.</strong> Research says roughly 70% of capability builds on the job. Where’s the coaching, the manager reinforcement, the practice? You’ve defaulted to the most expensive, least-scalable modality. The win-rate won’t move from a lecture hall.`,
                    coach_r2_digital:       `<strong>Digital scales cheaply, but on its own it won’t change deal behavior.</strong> Self-paced modules build awareness; consultative selling is a practiced skill. Pair the scale of digital with coaching for the deal teams and manager reinforcement, or the new behaviors never reach a live negotiation.`,
                    coach_r2_coachall:      `<strong>Equal coaching for all 1,400 is fair-sounding and unaffordable.</strong> High-impact coaching is your scarcest, most expensive resource — concentrate it where it moves the number, on the front-line deal teams, and scale the rest digitally. Spreading it thin wastes the one modality that changes behavior.`,
                    coach_r2_nocoach:       `<strong>Skipping coaching saves money and forfeits the behavior change.</strong> Consultative selling isn’t learned from a module — it’s coached in real deal reviews. Concentrate coaching on the teams that move the win-rate; that’s the 20 in 70-20-10 doing the heavy lifting.`,
                    coach_r2_nomanager:     `<strong>If the manager has no role, the learning evaporates the week after the course.</strong> Roughly 70% of capability sticks on the job — and it sticks when the manager coaches the deal reviews and observes the new behavior. Without them, you’re funding a classroom, not a capability.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>You’ve designed a program that costs more than the whole €4.2M budget.</strong> You’re about to walk into the board over budget while defending against a cut — that hands the CFO the argument. A 70-20-10 blend delivers more behavior change for less. Bring the spend back under €4.2M.`,
                    coach_r3_evidence:      `<strong>That’s a measurement case a board accepts.</strong> Level 4 business results, leading indicators that move first, a trained-vs-untrained cohort that isolates the effect, and an ROI grounded in the share of the €11M you can actually claim. You’ve moved from a happy sheet to evidence.`,
                    coach_r3_reaction:      `<strong>Your dashboard shows 4.6/5 satisfaction and 88% completion.</strong> My response: lovely — the services win-rate is still 18%. You measured whether people enjoyed the course, not whether they changed behavior or moved the number. Climb the ladder to behavior and business results, or this is a happy sheet.`,
                    coach_r3_learning:      `<strong>A post-course test proves they learned — it doesn’t prove they sell differently.</strong> Knowledge in a quiz isn’t behavior in a deal. Stopping at Level 2 still leaves the CFO asking what business number moved. Take it to behavior and results.`,
                    coach_r3_leadactivity:  `<strong>Courses delivered and hours of training are activity, not signal.</strong> They tell the board how busy L&D is, not whether the win-rate will move. Lead with skill assessments and manager-observed deal behaviors — the early indicators that the lagging business result will follow.`,
                    coach_r3_leadreaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction as your leading indicator is a happy sheet by another name.</strong> A high NPS on the training doesn’t predict a higher win-rate. Report the skill and behavior signals that actually run ahead of the business result.`,
                    coach_r3_claim:         `<strong>“Win-rate rose after we trained, so the training did it” is a story, not evidence.</strong> Sales also got a new pricing tool and a market tailwind the same quarter. Without a trained-vs-untrained comparison, I can’t credit the lift to you — and I won’t fund a story. Build the control group.`,
                    coach_r3_survey:        `<strong>Asking the sales team how much the training helped is self-report, not attribution.</strong> People over-credit what they just experienced. It’s softer than a cohort comparison and a CFO will discount it. Isolate the effect with a trained-vs-untrained control group.`,
                    coach_r3_vanity:        `<strong>A “€20 per €1” headline from hours and salary time saved is a vanity ROI.</strong> It’s not tied to the win-rate or the margin, and a finance audience sees through it in seconds. Ground the return in the share of the €11M your cohort evidence actually attributes.`,
                    coach_r3_noroi:         `<strong>“L&D impact can’t be quantified” is how the budget gets cut.</strong> If you won’t put a number on the return, finance will put their own number on the cost. You have a goal and an attribution method — use them to ground a credible ROI against the €11M.`,
                    coach_r4_business:      `<strong>Leading with the business case is the right move.</strong> The win-rate trajectory, the attributed margin and the €11M at stake are the language this board speaks. You’ve reframed L&D from a cost line to a lever on the number that matters.`,
                    coach_r4_effort:        `<strong>Effort and reach aren’t a result.</strong> How hard the team worked and how many people we reached tells me nothing about the win-rate. The board hears effort as the absence of impact. Lead with the business number.`,
                    coach_r4_activity:      `<strong>You opened with completion and satisfaction.</strong> My response: the services win-rate is still 18%. Activity is exactly what got the budget put on the chopping block. You had the business case — why are you leading with the happy sheet?`,
                    coach_r4_defend:        `<strong>That’s a defense, not a flinch.</strong> €4.2M funds the capability behind an €11M opportunity — cutting it defunds the transition the board says is its #1 priority. You’ve put the return on the table and answered the cut on its own terms.`,
                    coach_r4_invest:        `<strong>Bold — and you’ve earned the right to ask.</strong> With a goal-aligned program, a real measurement case and credible attribution, arguing for reinvestment is exactly the “Next CLO” move: the ROI on the program beats the savings from the cut. The board will fund a return.`,
                    coach_r4_investweak:    `<strong>Asking for more without the evidence to back it falls flat.</strong> Arguing for reinvestment only lands when the goal, the measurement and the attribution are solid — yours aren’t yet, so the CFO hears ambition without a return. You held the line on the budget, but the bolder ask needed a stronger case.`,
                    coach_r4_split:         `<strong>Giving back €0.7M to seem reasonable is a smaller version of the same mistake.</strong> You’ve still defunded part of the capability behind the €11M, just to look like a team player. If the return case is real, why concede any of it? Being agreeable isn’t strategic.`,
                    coach_r4_concede:       `<strong>You accepted the €1.4M cut to look fiscally responsible.</strong> But the transition you were funding is worth €11M in margin, and you just defunded the capability that delivers it. Being agreeable isn’t strategic. What’s the actual return on the budget you gave away?`,
                    coach_r4_happy:         `<strong>Committing to completion and satisfaction dashboards re-anchors L&D to the happy sheet.</strong> That’s the reporting the board already doesn’t trust. Commit to quarterly business KPIs — win-rate, margin, behavior — or you’ll be defending the budget all over again next year.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>“Keep improving and report back next year” is not a commitment.</strong> A board funds a leader who names the KPIs they’ll be held to and the sponsorship they need. Vague reassurance reads as no plan — and no plan is the easiest thing in the world to cut.`,
                    coach_r4_strong:        `<strong>That’s the Next CLO landing the case.</strong> You led with the business number, refused to defund the €11M capability, and committed to quarterly business KPIs and the sponsorship to make it stick. That’s L&D as an investment with a return — exactly what earns the strategic seat.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Anchored to the win-rate; capability mapped; catalogue pruned.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Goal, capability or catalogue not aligned to a business number.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `70-20-10 blend, front-line coaching, manager reinforcement, in budget.`,
                    sum_r2_classroom:       `Classroom default — expensive, low-scale, little behavior change.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Modality mix under-tuned for behavior change.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Level 4 results, behavior leading indicators, cohort attribution, grounded ROI.`,
                    sum_r3_happysheet:      `Measurement stopped at reaction / completion — a happy sheet.`,
                    sum_r3_noattr:          `Claimed credit with no control group — a story, not evidence.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Measurement case partially built.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Led with the business case; defended the budget; committed to KPIs.`,
                    sum_r4_conceded:        `Conceded the cut — defunded the €11M capability.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Board case under-framed.`,

                    insight_strategic:      `Strategic Partner: you ran the full strategic-L&D play. The program was anchored to the services win-rate — a single business metric — built on a 70-20-10 design that changed deal behavior, measured all the way to Level 4 results with a trained-vs-untrained cohort that isolated the effect, and defended in the board’s own language. You reframed L&D from a cost center to an investment with a return, and the budget held. This is the “Next CLO” earning the strategic seat.`,
                    insight_activity:       `Activity Trap: you anchored the program to an activity goal — completion or satisfaction — with no link to a business number. That is precisely why the budget was on the chopping block. However good the later design, a program tied to no business metric cannot be defended as strategic, and the creditable impact stayed near zero.`,
                    insight_classroom:      `Classroom Default: you over-invested in expensive, low-scale instructor-led training and under-used on-the-job practice, coaching and manager reinforcement. Capability builds ~70% on the job; a lecture-hall rollout burns budget and selling days while changing little behavior — so little of the €11M was ever unlocked.`,
                    insight_happysheet:     `Happy-Sheet Trap: you measured whether people enjoyed and completed the course, not whether they changed behavior or moved the number. The board has always had completion and satisfaction — and has never considered them evidence of value. Without climbing to behavior and business results, the case never became credible.`,
                    insight_noattr:         `Attribution Gap: you claimed the win-rate lift without a trained-vs-untrained comparison. With a new pricing tool and a market tailwind in the same quarter, the CFO can’t credit the lift to the program — so your ROI stayed a story, not evidence, and the impact you could defend was capped.`,
                    insight_conceded:       `Conceded the Cut: you accepted the €1.4M reduction to look fiscally responsible — and defunded the capability behind an €11M margin opportunity. Being agreeable isn’t strategic. You gave away the budget that funds the transition the board calls its #1 priority.`,
                    insight_conceded_note:  `You also conceded part of the cut — even a strong case loses force when you hand back the budget that funds the €11M opportunity.`,
                    insight_classroom_note: `Your modality mix leaned on the expensive classroom default, which limited the behavior change — and therefore the business impact — the program could deliver.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A partial case: parts of the strategic play landed, but the goal, the design, the measurement and the budget defense didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for a skeptical CFO to keep questioning whether L&D has truly become strategic.`,
                    insight_eval_low:       `Your measurement never climbed past Level 2 — you can show people reacted and learned, but not that they changed behavior or moved a business result. That is the credibility gap that keeps L&D a cost center.`,
                    insight_gave_away:      `You built a genuinely strategic case — aligned, evidenced, high-impact — and then gave back budget at the board. The case was strong enough to defend the full €4.2M (or win more); conceding any of it undercut the very investment logic you proved.`,

                    hint_activity:          `Your program is tied to an activity goal, so its creditable business impact is capped near zero. Re-anchor to a single business metric — the services win-rate — or the strategic case can’t be made.`,
